# Tutorial 10 — Choices and Branching
# Choice syntax (>), [jump], [goto], branch/merge patterns, and conditional gating.

@scenario {{SCENARIO_PREFIX}}_tutorial_10_menus
@include {{CHARACTERS_INCLUDE}}
{{BG_DECL}}@label start
[mode dialogue standard]
[set ui.dialogueUi project]
[set ui.textBoxAsset none]
[set ui.continueIndicator spinning_arrow]
[if tutorial_still]
[set ui.reduceMotion true]
[else]
[set ui.reduceMotion false]
[endif]
[if !tutorial_initialized]
[set tutorial_count 0]
[flag tutorial_initialized]
[endif]
[set ui.characterHeightFactor 1.5]
[set ui.characterBaselineY 1.5]
[set character.lavender.imageEffect none]
{{BG_START}}
[textspeed 28]
[autodelay 1650]
[show lavender center happy]
[wait 200]
Lavender: Every menu you've clicked in these tutorials is a choice. The syntax is one line:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}> Display text -> target_label{/color}
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: That's a {b}>{/b}, the button text, an arrow {b}->{/b}, and the label name to jump to.

@label lesson_menu
[show lavender far_left talking]
> Choice syntax -> section_basic
> Branch and merge pattern -> section_branching
> Gated (conditional) choices -> section_gated
> [jump] vs [goto] -> section_jump_goto
> Writing effective choice text -> section_writing
> Quick reference: choice flow -> quick_reference
> Practice: a branching scene -> practice_scene
> Back to Tutorial Hub -> return_hub

# ─── Quick Reference ─────────────────────────────────────────────

@label quick_reference
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Choices are labels plus visible options:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}> Ask about the key -> ask_key{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}> Leave quietly [if courage < 2] -> leave{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}@label ask_key{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[jump merge]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}@label merge{/color}
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Checklist: every target label exists, choices say what the player means, and branches merge when they should.
> Start the lesson sections -> section_basic
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Choice Syntax ───────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_basic
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: A choice block is a group of {b}>{/b} lines. The player sees them as buttons.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}Lavender: Where should we eat?{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}> The cafe downtown -> cafe_scene{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}> That new ramen place -> ramen_scene{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}> Let's just cook -> cook_scene{/color}
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: When the player clicks a button, execution jumps to the matching {b}@label{/b}.
Lavender: Every choice target must have a matching @label in the same file. Missing labels cause errors.
narrator: {b}Sweet spot:{/b} 2–4 choices. More than 4 overwhelms the player. Fewer than 2 isn't a choice.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Place choices after a character line — it reads naturally as "the character asks, the player answers."
> Next: branch and merge -> section_branching
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Branch and Merge ────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_branching
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: After a choice, the story {b}branches{/b}. The key design question: do branches stay separate or merge back?
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: {b}Merge pattern{/b} — each branch ends with [jump] to the same label:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}@label cafe_scene{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}Lavender: The cappuccino here is incredible.{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[jump after_dinner]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}@label ramen_scene{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}Lavender: Extra noodles, obviously.{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[jump after_dinner]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}@label after_dinner{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}Lavender: That was great. What's next?{/color}
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: Merging keeps the story manageable. You don't maintain parallel plotlines for every minor choice.
Lavender: {b}Split pattern{/b} — branches never rejoin. Use this only for major route decisions (ending paths, character routes).
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Rule of thumb: 90% of choices should merge. 10% should split. Never split unless it's a major story fork.
> Next: gated choices -> section_gated
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Gated Choices ───────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_gated
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Wrap a choice in {b}[if]/[endif]{/b} to show it only when a condition is met:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}> Go to the park -> park_scene{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[if has_key == 1]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}> Unlock the shed -> shed_scene{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[endif]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}> Stay home -> home_scene{/color}
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: Players without the key see two choices. Players with the key see three.
Lavender: Gated choices reward exploration — the player earned access to a new option through earlier actions.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Pair this with Tutorial 07's [set]/[inc] system: choices set variables, variables gate future choices.
> Next: jump vs goto -> section_jump_goto
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── [jump] vs [goto] ───────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_jump_goto
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: Two navigation commands:
narrator: {b}[jump label]{/b} — moves to a label in the {b}same file{/b}.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[jump after_dinner]{/color}
narrator: {b}[goto scenario:label]{/b} — moves to a label in a {b}different scenario file{/b}.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[goto chapter_2:start]{/color}
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Use [jump] for everything inside one file — choice targets, merge points, loops.
Lavender: Use [goto] for cross-file navigation — changing chapters, returning to a hub, loading a new scene.
narrator: The scenario name comes from the {b}@scenario{/b} declaration at the top of the target file.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: This tutorial uses [jump] everywhere internally and [goto] only to return to the hub.
> Next: writing choice text -> section_writing
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Writing Choice Text ────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_writing
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: The text on a choice button shapes the player's expectation. Write it carefully.
narrator: {b}Be specific:{/b} "Climb the fire escape" beats "Go up."
narrator: {b}Hint at tone:{/b} "Confront him (risky)" vs "Ask him calmly."
narrator: {b}Match the voice:{/b} if the protagonist is sarcastic, the choices should sound sarcastic.
narrator: {b}Keep it short:{/b} one line per choice. If it wraps in the button, it's too long.
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: Avoid {b}false choices{/b} — two options that lead to the same outcome. Players notice.
Lavender: Even small branches should differ. A single [inc] or different narrator line is enough to make a choice feel real.
> Practice: a branching scene -> practice_scene
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Practice ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

@label practice_scene
[set kindness 0]
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: A rainy afternoon. You're in a cafe. Someone left an umbrella on the chair next to you.
> Take it to the front counter -> pc_counter
> Leave it — not your problem -> pc_leave
> Wait to see if someone comes back -> pc_wait

@label pc_counter
[inc kindness]
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: The barista takes it with a nod. "Thanks — people forget these every day."
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[inc kindness]{/color} — this choice incremented a variable.
[jump pc_merge]

@label pc_leave
[show lavender center talking]
narrator: The umbrella sits there. Eventually another customer picks it up and walks out.
narrator: No [inc] — the variable stays unchanged. The story remembers inaction too.
[jump pc_merge]

@label pc_wait
[inc kindness]
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Ten minutes pass. A woman rushes in, soaked, and spots the umbrella.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: "Oh, thank goodness!" She gives you a grateful look.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[inc kindness]{/color} — patience was rewarded.
[jump pc_merge]

@label pc_merge
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: All three branches just merged here at {b}pc_merge{/b}. The story continues as one path.
[if kindness >= 1]
Lavender: But the variable remembers: kindness is now 1. Future [if] blocks can react to that.
[else]
Lavender: Kindness stayed at 0. A future [if kindness >= 1] check would skip its content.
[endif]
Lavender: That's the complete choice lifecycle: present options, branch, optionally track state, merge.
> Replay the scene -> practice_scene
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

@label return_hub
[if !tutorial_done_10]
[flag tutorial_done_10]
[inc tutorial_count]
[endif]
[goto {{HUB_TARGET}}:topics_page_2]
